::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which behavior best supports discussion?
{
=A. Listening before responding#Correct. Listening first supports accurate response.
~B. Interrupting often#Interrupting weakens discussion.
~C. Ignoring the speaker#Ignoring is not active listening.
~D. Changing the topic#Changing topics avoids the message.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which tone best fits asking a teacher for help?
{
~A. Sarcastic#Sarcasm does not fit a help request.
~B. Demanding#Demanding tone may sound rude.
=C. Respectful#Correct. A teacher help request should be respectful.
~D. Careless#Careless tone is unclear.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which word is most academic for information that supports a claim?
{
~A. stuff#This is vague.
=B. evidence#Correct. Evidence supports a claim.
~C. thing#This is vague.
~D. whatever#This is informal.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which question best clarifies an assignment?
{
~A. This is impossible.#This does not ask a specific question.
~B. Why is this confusing?#This is too broad.
~C. Can someone do it for me?#This asks for completion, not clarification.
=D. Should I submit one paragraph or two?#Correct. This asks about a specific expectation.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "Our survey showed that most students want more practice time." Which appeal is strongest?
{
~A. Emotion#The excerpt does not focus on feelings.
=B. Logic#Correct. The speaker uses survey evidence.
~C. Trust#No role or experience is named.
~D. Confusion#Confusion is not a rhetorical appeal.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which word means the people receiving a message?
{
=A. audience#Correct. Audience means the listeners or viewers.
~B. purpose#Purpose is the reason for communicating.
~C. evidence#Evidence supports a claim.
~D. transition#A transition connects ideas.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
What does paraphrase mean?
{
~A. Copy exact words#That is repeating, not paraphrasing.
~B. Change the idea#That changes meaning.
~C. Ignore the speaker#That is not listening.
=D. Restate in your own words#Correct. A paraphrase keeps the meaning in new words.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Why does audience matter?
{
~A. It does not affect communication.#Audience strongly affects communication.
~B. Every audience needs the same message.#Messages should fit audiences.
=C. It helps the speaker choose words and tone.#Correct. Audience guides communication choices.
~D. Audience only matters in writing.#Audience matters in speaking too.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best builds on an idea?
{
=A. Your point about audience connects to our opening; we could add a question there.#Correct. This connects and extends the idea.
~B. I was not listening.#This does not build.
~C. Stop talking.#This blocks discussion.
~D. That idea is useless.#This is disrespectful.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Which appeal focuses on credibility?
{
~A. Emotion#Emotion focuses on feelings.
=B. Trust#Correct. Trust focuses on credibility.
~C. Logic#Logic focuses on reasons and evidence.
~D. Tone#Tone is attitude, not an appeal type here.
}
